ed in his pivotal, albeit temporary, conversion to Christianity, making him the first East Saxon monarch to embrace the new faith. This act was largely influenced by his uncle, Æthelberht of Kent, the powerful Bretwalda who championed the Gregorian mission in Anglo-Saxon England, positioning Sæberht's kingdom, particularly its principal settlement of London, at the forefront of early Christianisation efforts.

